[
  {
    "name": "VCE",
    "evidence": "The flame flashed back through the duct to the chamber and ignited the remaining EO, resulting in a powerful explosion. The explosion occurred shortly after 2 PM on August 19, 2004. ... The location and magnitude of the damage to the ventilation system and Chamber 7 indicate that explosive EO-laden air was transported through the ventilation ducting to the oxidizer where it initially ignited. ... The force of the explosion sheared the hinges and propelled both chamber doors outward.",
    "explanation": "A vapor cloud explosion (VCE) occurred when an explosive concentration of ethylene oxide (EO) vapor was ignited in the oxidizer and chamber, resulting in a powerful explosion and overpressure damage. The mechanism matches a VCE: a flammable vapor cloud (EO) accumulated and was ignited, causing a destructive blast."
  },
  {
    "name": "Confined explosion",
    "evidence": "The flame front, entering the chamber through the open backvent, initiated a powerful explosion. The force of the explosion sheared the hinges and propelled both chamber doors outward. ... The pressure flexed the entire chamber structure outward, leaving it permanently disfigured.",
    "explanation": "The explosion occurred within the confined space of the sterilizer chamber and its ventilation system, resulting in overpressure that damaged the chamber and propelled doors outward. This matches the mechanism of a confined explosion: ignition of a flammable atmosphere within an enclosure causing structural damage."
  },
  {
    "name": "Toxicity dispersion",
    "evidence": "EO immediately began to leak out of the chamber door into the building, causing nearby LEL monitors to alarm. ... The Ontario Police Department immediately evacuated neighboring businesses and closed an approximately one quarter mile section of the street on the east side of the plant. ... Soon thereafter, the fire department began monitoring the air for EO and explosive gases using detector tubes and a direct-reading gas analyzer. ... No explosive concentrations were detected, but levels in the 1.0 ppm range were detected inside the building. ... because of elevated EO levels and structural damage, the fire department continued to monitor for EO and restrict access into the facility.",
    "explanation": "Ethylene oxide (EO), a toxic gas, was released into the building and detected at hazardous levels, prompting air monitoring, evacuation, and restricted access. This matches the mechanism of toxicity dispersion: release and spread of a toxic substance in the environment."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name": "Confined explosion",
    "evidence": "As the steam heated the trapped peroxide, it exothermically decomposed and overpressurized the pipe, causing it to rupture (Figure 1). The pressure concussion also caused additional damage to equipment in the area.",
    "explanation": "The peroxide/alcohol mixture decomposed inside a confined pipe, leading to overpressure and pipe rupture—this matches the mechanism of a confined explosion."
  },
  {
    "name": "Flash fire",
    "evidence": "The venting flammable vapor then ignited, resulting in a flash fire that burned two employees and significantly damaged nearby equipment (Figure 2).",
    "explanation": "The release and ignition of flammable vapor resulted in a flash fire, as explicitly described in the incident."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name": "VCE",
    "evidence": "The gasoline spray aerosolized, forming a large vapor cloud, which ignited after reaching an ignition source in the wastewater treatment (WWT) area of the facility. The blast and fire from multiple secondary explosions resulted in significant damage to 17 of the 48 petroleum storage tanks and other equipment onsite and in neighborhoods and businesses offsite. The blast created a pressure wave registering 2.9 on the Richter scale1 and damaging approximately 300 homes and businesses up to 1.25 miles from the site.",
    "explanation": "A vapor cloud explosion (VCE) occurred when the large gasoline vapor cloud formed from the tank overflow ignited, producing a blast wave and widespread damage, which matches the mechanism of a VCE."
  },
  {
    "name": "Pool fire",
    "evidence": "After the explosion, fuel in the damaged tanks burned for over two days while emergency responders fought to control the fire and prevent other tanks from igniting. The large fire demanded emergency personnel and resources from across the Commonwealth of Puerto Rico and the US mainland. Local fire departments with assistance from an industrial firefighting company took 66 hours to extinguish the fire after the explosion. As a result, 17 of the 48 tanks burned.",
    "explanation": "The overflowed gasoline pooled in secondary containment and other low-lying areas, and after ignition, resulted in large pool fires as the spilled fuel burned for days."
  },
  {
    "name": "Flash fire",
    "evidence": "Onsite security video captured the ignition and initial flash fire in the WWT area occurring seconds before the explosion.",
    "explanation": "The ignition of the vapor cloud produced a flash fire prior to the explosion, consistent with the mechanism of a flash fire (rapid combustion of a flammable vapor cloud)."
  },
  {
    "name": "Toxicity dispersion",
    "evidence": "The incident forced the Commonwealth government and local officials to evacuate approximately 3,000 people in a nearby prison and other government facilities. Changing wind patterns caused the governor to prepare for the evacuation of over 30,000 individuals likely affected by particulate fallout from the smoke plume that extended miles out to sea. ... The CAPECO incident released thousands of gallons of oil, fire suppression foam, and contaminated runoff to Malaria Creek, which traverses the Puente Blanco community to the San Juan Bay. ... Environmental assessments jointly conducted by the EPA, the US Fish and Wildlife Service (USFWS), and the Puerto Rico Department of Natural Resources (PR DNR) found dead wildlife and both aquatic and avian species, including several legally protected species, covered in oil.",
    "explanation": "The incident resulted in the release and dispersion of toxic substances (smoke, oil, contaminated runoff) into the air, water, and environment, causing environmental and potential health impacts, matching the mechanism of toxicity dispersion."
  },
  {
    "name": "Asphyxiation",
    "evidence": "At the 12 a.m. check, operations staff noticed a fog on the ground and on the road along Tanks 504, 411 and 409. Fuel gushed from the vents, creating a spray of gasoline that formed a vapor cloud and pooled in the secondary containment dike. ... At midnight, the tank farm operator started to perform the hourly check of Tank 409, but before reaching the tank, he observed a vapor cloud and a strong smell of gasoline. ... Noting the potential danger, the supervisor sent one operator to the security gate, while the supervisor and another operator drove around the facility attempting to find the source of the leak and developing vapor cloud.",
    "explanation": "A large, dense gasoline vapor cloud formed and spread at ground level, creating an asphyxiation hazard due to displacement of oxygen in the affected areas, as evidenced by the fog and strong gasoline odor."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name": "VCE",
    "evidence": "The initial explosion occurred inside a vertical above-ground storage tank that was being filled with Varnish Makers’ and Painters’ (VM&P) naphtha. ... The explosion sent the VM&P tank rocketing into the air, trailing a cloud of smoke and fire from the burning liquid; it landed approximately 130 feet away. Witnesses heard the explosion and saw the fireball from several miles away. Within moments, two more tanks ruptured and released their contents into the rapidly escalating fire that was concentrated inside the earthen spill containment area surrounding the tank farm.",
    "explanation": "A vapor cloud explosion (VCE) is indicated by the ignition of a flammable vapor-air mixture inside the tank, resulting in a powerful explosion that destroyed tanks and caused widespread damage. The mechanism matches a VCE: formation and ignition of a flammable vapor cloud in a confined or semi-confined space."
  },
  {
    "name": "Fire ball",
    "evidence": "The explosion sent the VM&P tank rocketing into the air, trailing a cloud of smoke and fire from the burning liquid; it landed approximately 130 feet away. Witnesses heard the explosion and saw the fireball from several miles away.",
    "explanation": "A fireball is produced when a large quantity of flammable vapor is ignited, resulting in a rapidly expanding ball of fire. The description of a visible fireball seen from miles away after the tank explosion matches this mechanism."
  },
  {
    "name": "Pool fire",
    "evidence": "Within moments, two more tanks ruptured and released their contents into the rapidly escalating fire that was concentrated inside the earthen spill containment area surrounding the tank farm.3 Approximately 20,000 gallons of flammable liquid were released into the spill containment.",
    "explanation": "A pool fire occurs when flammable liquid is released and ignited, burning in a pool. The release of 20,000 gallons of flammable liquid into a containment area and subsequent fire matches the pool fire mechanism."
  },
  {
    "name": "Jet fire",
    "evidence": "The explosion sent the VM&P tank rocketing into the air, trailing a cloud of smoke and fire from the burning liquid; it landed approximately 130 feet away.",
    "explanation": "A jet fire is a burning stream of flammable liquid or vapor under pressure. The description of the tank rocketing through the air, trailing burning liquid, indicates a pressurized release and ignition, matching the jet fire mechanism."
  },
  {
    "name": "Flash fire",
    "evidence": "The initial explosion occurred inside a vertical above-ground storage tank that was being filled with Varnish Makers’ and Painters’ (VM&P) naphtha. ... The tank contained an ignitable vapor-air mixture in its head space. ... The energy from a static spark would have been adequate to ignite this vapor-air mixture.",
    "explanation": "A flash fire is the ignition of a flammable vapor cloud, resulting in a brief, intense fire. The ignition of a vapor-air mixture inside the tank by a static spark matches the flash fire mechanism."
  }
]

[
  {
    "name": "Toxicity dispersion",
    "evidence": "On the morning of August 14, 2002, 48,000 pounds of chlorine was released over a 3-hour period during a railroad tank car unloading operation at DPC Enterprises, L.P., near Festus, Missouri. ... Although the wind direction on the day of the release carried the majority of the chlorine plume away from neighboring residential areas, some areas were evacuated. Sixty-three people from the surrounding community sought medical evaluation at the local hospital for respiratory distress, and three were admitted for overnight observation. The release affected hundreds of other nearby residents and employees, and the community was advised to shelter-in-place for 4 hours. ... The chlorine plume moved primarily east-to-southeast in the direction of Goodwin Brothers Construction and Intermodal Tire, though it is likely that it migrated into the adjacent Blue Fountain mobile home park when there was no wind. As indicated by dispersion modeling, chlorine concentrations of 3 ppm could have extended to approximately 3.7 miles from the point of release (Trinity Consultants, 2003).",
    "explanation": "A large quantity of toxic chlorine gas was released and dispersed into the atmosphere, resulting in a toxic plume that exposed the community, caused respiratory distress, and required medical attention and shelter-in-place orders. This matches the mechanism of 'Toxicity dispersion' as defined in the hazard consequence list."
  },
  {
    "name": "Asphyxiation",
    "evidence": "Chlorine is a toxic chemical. Concentrations as low as 10 parts per million are classified as 'immediately dangerous to life or health' (NIOSH, 2003). ... Sixty-three people from the surrounding community sought medical evaluation at the local hospital for respiratory distress, and three were admitted for overnight observation. ... HAZMAT responders measured chlorine concentrations of greater than 1,000 ppm at the release site, which is life threatening without proper respiratory equipment.",
    "explanation": "The incident resulted in atmospheric concentrations of chlorine that are immediately dangerous to life or health, causing respiratory distress and posing a risk of asphyxiation to exposed individuals. This matches the mechanism of 'Asphyxiation' due to displacement of breathable air and direct toxic effects on the respiratory system."
  }
]

